<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-right-triangle-trigonometry">
  <title>Right Triangle Trigonometry</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-right-triangle-trigonometry">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Identify the legs, the hypotenuse, and the two complementary acute angles of a right triangle, and use the Pythagorean theorem to find a missing side.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain why the ratios of side lengths in a right triangle depend only on the acute angle <m>\theta</m>, and use them to define <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, and <m>\tan(\theta)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Draw and label a right triangle for a physical situation, decide which trigonometric ratio relates the known and unknown quantities, and solve for the unknown length.</p></li>
      <li><p>Describe the unit triangles in which the hypotenuse, the adjacent side, or the opposite side has length <m>1</m>, and identify the remaining side lengths as trigonometric values.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize the three Pythagorean identities as the Pythagorean theorem applied to the three unit triangles.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Trigonometry begins with the study of right triangles. Soon we will extend these ideas to
      angles of any size by using the unit circle, and then to functions that we can differentiate
      and integrate. Today we build the foundation: what the trigonometric ratios are, why they
      make sense, and how they let us measure things that are hard to reach directly.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-anatomy" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Anatomy of a Right Triangle</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A <term>right triangle</term> has one angle of <m>90^\circ</m>, called the right angle.
        The two sides that meet at the right angle are the <term>legs</term>. The third side,
        opposite the right angle, is the <term>hypotenuse</term>. The other two angles are less than
        <m>90^\circ</m> and are called <term>acute</term> angles.
      </p>
      <p>
        Throughout this activity we draw the right angle on the right side of the triangle, at the
        vertex <m>C</m>. The angle at <m>B</m> is <m>\theta</m>, the angle at <m>A</m> is
        <m>\phi</m>, and the side lengths are labeled <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> as shown.
      </p>
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          A right triangle with the right angle at vertex C on the lower right, vertex B at the
          lower left, and vertex A at the top directly above C. The angle at B is labeled theta
          and the angle at A is labeled phi. The side from B to C has length a, the side from C to
          A has length b, and the hypotenuse from B to A has length c.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \coordinate (B) at (0,0);
            \coordinate (C) at (5,0);
            \coordinate (A) at (5,3);
            \draw[thick] (B) -- (C) -- (A) -- cycle;
            \draw (4.6,0) -- (4.6,0.4) -- (5,0.4);
            \node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
            \node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
            \node[above right] at (A) {$A$};
            \node at (1.0,0.28) {$\theta$};
            \node at (4.75,2.3) {$\phi$};
            \node[below] at (2.5,0) {$a$};
            \node[right] at (5,1.5) {$b$};
            \node[above left] at (2.5,1.5) {$c$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-complementary" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\theta=35^\circ</m>. Find <m>\phi</m>. Then explain why, in every right triangle,
          the two acute angles satisfy <m>\theta+\phi=90^\circ</m>. (Two angles that add to
          <m>90^\circ</m> are called <term>complementary</term>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-rt-pythagorean">
    <title>The Pythagorean Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In a right triangle with legs <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> and hypotenuse <m>c</m>,
        <md>a^2+b^2=c^2\text{.}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-pythagorean-use" workspace="0in">
    <title>Finding a Missing Side</title>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-pythagorean-missing" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A right triangle has a leg of length <m>a=5</m> and a hypotenuse of length <m>c=13</m>. Use
          <xref ref="thm-rt-pythagorean"/> to find the other leg <m>b</m>. Then find the
          lengths of the legs and hypotenuse of a triangle with the same shape that is
          twice as large.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-ratios" workspace="0in">
    <title>Similar Triangles and Trigonometric Ratios</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Any two right triangles that share the same acute angle <m>\theta</m> are similar: one is
        an rescaled copy of the other. Similar triangles have the same ratios between
        their side lengths. Let us see this in a specific case. Consider a right triangle with
        <m>a=4</m>, <m>b=3</m>, and <m>c=5</m>, with the angle <m>\theta</m> at <m>B</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-similar-copy" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A second right triangle has the same angle <m>\theta</m> but a hypotenuse of length
          <m>15</m>. Find its legs. Then, for each of the two triangles, compute the three ratios
          <m>\dfrac{b}{c}</m>, <m>\dfrac{a}{c}</m>, and <m>\dfrac{b}{a}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-ratio-depends-on-angle" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What do you notice about the ratios for the two triangles? Explain why the ratios depend
          on the angle <m>\theta</m> but not on how large the triangle is.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <definition xml:id="def-rt-trigonometric-ratios">
    <title>The Trigonometric Ratios</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For an acute angle <m>\theta</m> in a right triangle, the side across from <m>\theta</m> is
        the <term>opposite</term> side, and the leg that touches <m>\theta</m> is the
        <term>adjacent</term> side. The <term>sine</term>, <term>cosine</term>, and
        <term>tangent</term> of <m>\theta</m> are the ratios
        <md>
          <mrow> \sin(\theta) \amp = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{b}{c} </mrow>
          <mrow> \cos(\theta) \amp = \frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{a}{c} </mrow>
          <mrow> \tan(\theta) \amp = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}} = \frac{b}{a} </mrow>
        </md>
        where the last expression in each line refers to the triangle labeled in
        <xref ref="ex-right-triangle-anatomy"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-two-angles" workspace="0in">
    <title>One Triangle, Two Angles</title>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-ratios-both-angles" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the triangle with <m>a=4</m>, <m>b=3</m>, and <m>c=5</m>, find <m>\sin(\theta)</m>,
          <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, and <m>\tan(\theta)</m>. Then find <m>\sin(\phi)</m>,
          <m>\cos(\phi)</m>, and <m>\tan(\phi)</m>. Be careful: which side is
          opposite and which is adjacent changes when you switch angles.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-rt-cofunction" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which of your six values are equal to one another? Use the fact that <m>\phi=90^\circ-\theta</m> to
          write what you found as a statement about <m>\sin(\theta)</m> and
          <m>\cos(90^\circ-\theta)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-kite" workspace="0in">
    <title>Applications: A Kite on a String</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Trigonometric ratios let us find lengths that are difficult to measure directly. For the
        problems below, use a calculator in degree mode.
      </p>
      <p>
        A kite is flying so that the string makes an angle of <m>70^\circ</m> with the ground. The
        string is <m>100</m> feet long. How high is the kite above the ground?
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-kite-diagram" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw and label the right triangle. Which side is the length of the string? Which angle
          is <m>70^\circ</m>? Which side is the height that we want to find?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-kite-ratio" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which two sides of the triangle are involved, one known and one unknown? Which
          trigonometric ratio relates them? Write the equation and solve for the height of the kite.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-right-triangle-flagpole" workspace="0in">
    <title>Applications: A Flagpole</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When you stand <m>10</m> feet away from a flagpole, you must look up at an angle of
        <m>50^\circ</m> above the horizontal to see the top of the pole. Your eyes are exactly
        <m>5</m> feet above the ground. How tall is the flagpole?
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-flag-diagram" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw the right triangle. Label the known and unknown lengths and the known angle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-flag-ratio" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which trigonometric ratio involves the two sides in question? Write the equation and solve
          for the unknown length.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-flag-eye-level" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the length you just found the height of the flagpole? If not, use the height of your
          eyes to finish the problem.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-rt-unit-triangles">
    <title>Unit Triangles</title>
    <p>
      Because similar triangles share the same ratios, we may rescale a right triangle to any size
      we like without changing its angle <m>\theta</m>. It is especially useful to rescale so that
      one of the three sides has length <m>1</m>. The result is a <term>unit triangle</term>. When
      <m>\theta</m> is acute, the other two sides of a unit triangle are trigonometric values.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>If the hypotenuse is <m>1</m>, then the adjacent side is <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and the
        opposite side is <m>\sin(\theta)</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>If the adjacent side is <m>1</m>, then the hypotenuse is the <term>secant</term>,
        <m>\sec(\theta)=\dfrac{c}{a}=\dfrac{1}{\cos(\theta)}</m>, and the opposite side is
        <m>\tan(\theta)</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>If the opposite side is <m>1</m>, then the hypotenuse is the <term>cosecant</term>,
        <m>\csc(\theta)=\dfrac{c}{b}=\dfrac{1}{\sin(\theta)}</m>, and the adjacent side is the
        <term>cotangent</term>, <m>\cot(\theta)=\dfrac{a}{b}=\dfrac{1}{\tan(\theta)}</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-rt-three-unit-triangles">
    <title>The Three Unit Triangles</title>
    <image width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Three right triangles side by side, each with the acute angle theta at the lower left and
        the right angle at the lower right. In the first, the hypotenuse is 1, the horizontal leg
        is cosine of theta, and the vertical leg is sine of theta. In the second, the horizontal
        leg is 1, the vertical leg is tangent of theta, and the hypotenuse is secant of theta. In
        the third, the vertical leg is 1, the horizontal leg is cotangent of theta, and the
        hypotenuse is cosecant of theta.
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\th}{40}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\s}{2.6}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\cs}{\s*cos(\th)}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\sn}{\s*sin(\th)}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\tn}{\s*tan(\th)}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\ct}{\s/tan(\th)}
          \begin{scope}
            \coordinate (B) at (0,0); \coordinate (C) at (\cs,0); \coordinate (A) at (\cs,\sn);
            \draw[thick] (B)--(C)--(A)--cycle;
            \draw (\cs-0.25,0)--(\cs-0.25,0.25)--(\cs,0.25);
            \node at (0.75,0.18) {$\theta$};
            \node[below] at (\cs/2,0) {$\cos(\theta)$};
            \node[right] at (\cs,\sn/2) {$\sin(\theta)$};
            \node[above left] at (\cs/2,\sn/2) {$1$};
          \end{scope}
          \begin{scope}[xshift=3.6cm]
            \coordinate (B) at (0,0); \coordinate (C) at (\s,0); \coordinate (A) at (\s,\tn);
            \draw[thick] (B)--(C)--(A)--cycle;
            \draw (\s-0.25,0)--(\s-0.25,0.25)--(\s,0.25);
            \node at (0.75,0.18) {$\theta$};
            \node[below] at (\s/2,0) {$1$};
            \node[right] at (\s,\tn/2) {$\tan(\theta)$};
            \node[above left] at (\s/2,\tn/2) {$\sec(\theta)$};
          \end{scope}
          \begin{scope}[xshift=8cm]
            \coordinate (B) at (0,0); \coordinate (C) at (\ct,0); \coordinate (A) at (\ct,\s);
            \draw[thick] (B)--(C)--(A)--cycle;
            \draw (\ct-0.25,0)--(\ct-0.25,0.25)--(\ct,0.25);
            \node at (0.75,0.18) {$\theta$};
            \node[below] at (\ct/2,0) {$\cot(\theta)$};
            \node[right] at (\ct,\s/2) {$1$};
            \node[above left] at (\ct/2,\s/2) {$\csc(\theta)$};
          \end{scope}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-rt-pythagorean-identities">
    <title>The Pythagorean Identities</title>
    <p>
      Applying <xref ref="thm-rt-pythagorean"/> to each of the three unit triangles gives
      one identity apiece. Each holds for every acute angle <m>\theta</m>, and we will see that
      they hold for every angle where the expressions are defined.
      <md>
        <mrow> \sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta) \amp = 1 </mrow>
        <mrow> \tan^2(\theta)+1 \amp = \sec^2(\theta) </mrow>
        <mrow> 1+\cot^2(\theta) \amp = \csc^2(\theta) </mrow>
      </md>
      The first comes from the triangle with hypotenuse <m>1</m>, the second from the triangle
      with adjacent side <m>1</m>, and the third from the triangle with opposite side <m>1</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
</worksheet>
